ime we  also see a very strong reduction of the  or complete embolism actually of the  induction of dna damage  strongly suggesting that the dna damage  induction that we see  is secondary and is caused by by  by the oxidative stress in the cells  in contrast a second example is is  mms a methylating agent intox  again induces oxidative stress but also  activates in this case both of the dna  damage reporters and also p53 again  it's also here the question again is of  course is the dna damage  induction that we see is that caused by  reaction with the dna  or is it secondary to oxidative stress  where we run the tox tracker again  in the presence of those ros scavengers  in the absence mms causes oxidative  stress  as well as dna damage  are we adding the glutathio anesthetic  cysteine  oxidative stress is strongly reduced but  also the induction of  but but the industrial dna damage is not  affected suggesting that mms directly  binds to the dna  and the dna damage is not caused  secondarily to oxidative stress  which is in line with with with the  literature because mms  is a methylating agent that methylates  dna and is also a mutagen and in  vivo carcinogen um  when we expose the cells in the presence  of glutathione also daily oxidative  stress  is reduced but now we do see a reduction  of of the dna damage response and that  is because  glutathione binds directly to mms so  here we're not scavenging  the reactive oxygen species but we're  actually rather scavenging mms itself  so there's always some causes by  interpreting these  these data then to summarize just some  practical facts about the essay  so for pharmaceuticals typically the top  concentration is one millimolar  chemicals up to 10 millimolar in line  with the  recommendations from the different  regulatory agencies  either we require about five to ten  milligrams for for pharmaceuticals fifty  two hundred milligrams for chemicals if  we need to go up to that high  concentration  standard turnaround time is about two  weeks although there's a  express service where we can deliver a  report in three days  uh typically we can test let's say  between one and twenty five to thirty  compounds per week  and the sa is compatible with the usual  solvents like dmso pbs water ethanol  the essays have been shown to be  compatible with the right  range of materials obviously small  molecules  but also polymers complex mixtures  nanomaterials  and also all kind of intermediates that  are tested for occupational toxicology  a talk tracker is available  as a service from toxis but  a stock tracker but also toxic raise it  is also available through  charles river as a partner and by using  these outsourcing platforms like  scientist.com  the talk tracker is also available as a  kit so labs can buy  toxic kits to run it in their own  facilities  and we we always have the opportunity to  set up the multi-year licenses to really  fully support integration of this essay  in the laboratory  and with that i would like to thank you  thank you dr roy and dr hendricks for  your informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you would like to  ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  let's get started our first question  is  are you planning to have a talks tracker  lab training course later this year  yes so this is phil hendricks um yes  there are plans  uh different plans actually to have a  tax tracker lab training  there was actually already scheduled for  last year but then obviously due to the  covert situation that got postponed but  there are two activities that we are  that we are working on one is a  uh this is a lab training actually  in our lab facilities in the netherlands  uh and the second uh uh thing that we're  working on actually together with  miltoni  is if possible host a practical workshop  alongside the eurotox conference that  will be held later this year  that will happen in copenhagen  but of course it depends a bit on the  other covert situation how that will  develop  but once possible yes there will  definitely be possibilities for a  hands-on training  either in our facilities or at the  external side  thank you dr hendricks our next question  what is the rationale for using mouth  stem cells versus human  or non-stem cells  yeah there are various answers to that  question  we from the start decided if we want to  capture  the cellular responses related to  genotoxicity we need a cell system  that is basically  [Music]  reflecting normal cells so we didn't  want to use cancer cell lines or  transform cells  so primary cells is the way to go but  obviously they are not very well  maintained in the lab and we can  definitely not modify it and so stem  cells  turned out to be a very good platform  for this  for this subject by the time that we  started the development of tox tracker  the uh the human steps of human  embryonic stem cells were available but  there were obviously all kind of ethical  reasons  uh not to use these cells and human  induced pluripotent stem cells  they were simply not invented in those  days so um  so for that reason we we made use of  mouse embryonic stem cells  uh also because there was a huge number  a huge amount of information available  from these data  toxicogenomics mutation data  genetically very stable nicely deployed  i have a normal cell cycle control in  our dna repair proficient  and in due course we obviously discussed  whether we should switch to human cells  especially after the let's say the the  improvements on the  induced pluripotent stem cells um  but we decided to stick with with the  mouse cells i think these are  very well fit for purpose  the system is able to identify around 95  of all human in vivo genotoxins  so there's really no nothing much to  gain  it's not simply possible to transfer the  reporters to human cells  so we would really have to start from  scratch and of course with the  validation and the ongoing always  evaluation constructor it would be a  large step back but i said fit for  purpose i think in this case  is the reason the only area where mouse  and human might differ  is in metabolic metabolism so  metabolization by liver instance for  instance on both human and mouse stem  cells do not have a lot of  metabolization  activity so we have to rely on on an  external metabolization source anyway  so the simple answers yes we thought  about it but i think  there's a lot of good reason to stick  with the mouse cells  we have no indication that the situation  or the sensitivity will improve  by switching to human cells in this case  okay thank you dr hendricks we have  another question  is the talks tracker suitable for other  flow cytometers  yes that is uh that's a fair question  and the answer is yes  so as far we are  running essay also on the on different  brands of flow cytometers  and as part of the oecd validation the  external labs  are also using different brands ranging  so far i have to say we have not  encountered a flowcharter  that is not compatible with the  wiki system of course there are  differences in speed  and ease of use and the software that is  associated  yeah so there are different differences  but the sa seems to be compatible with  basically the whole system as long as  they have a  96 well or ideally even a 384 well  reading capacity but the speed differs  definitely between those systems  okay great thank you again and another  question  any measures regarding mitochondrial dna  uh no no i don't see it now  uh the uh  um so we have we have not been able in  previous studies  not used to this so is talks tracker  capable of analyzing a mixture sample  such as hemp extract  which may induce more than one type of  stress or stresses  um yes and no so yes definitely  we've tested we and others have tested  uh  a wide range of different mixtures uh  from let's say artificially  reconstructed mixtures with a few  compounds  up to very high complexity mixtures like  like oil extracts which really contain  more than a million different molecules  and i think in most of the cases it was  definitely  possible to to detect um  genotoxic pollutants or genotoxic  factors in those mixtures  um the sensitivity of the sa is is high  so that means that also small fractions  of pollutants or  genotoxic constituents could be  identified  obviously it is not possible to identify  which compound in there is causing a  certain effect  but it's definitely possible to detect  uh for instance the induction both  oxidative stress dna damage  um and then i think using extension  protocols or  or let's say using for instance  [Music]  protocols that also include kinetics of  reporter activation  it is definitely possible to detect  different mechanisms of action  but obviously again it's it's impossible  to then determine which compound or  which fraction in that mixture is  causing the  the effect  okay thank you so much dr hendrix let's  see  i've got time for one more question here  um  has the assay only been validated using  small molecules  and chemicals or were there also other  types of materials tested like  nanoparticles mixtures or natural  extracts  yeah that's a good and a fair question  obviously most of the validation work  has been done with  with with pure small molecules with pure  chemicals  um there there's quite extensive  data from the tox tracker system  and nanomaterials by others but also we  have been involved in the number of  collaborations with different labs in  the world and most of the work is  actually published  the system seems to be very compatible  with nanomaterials  and we've always taken care that we  measure the actual cellular uptake and  look at intercellular localization and  that seems to work very well  we've definitely also did some mixture  studies  some of the data had been published  mostly on posters  for instance the sot to get  both for for mixtures related to  to e-liquids  [Music]  environmental samples and petroleum  products  um we've had a little experience with  testing  other modalities like  like proteins or oligonucleotides but  that  that data is very limited in all honesty  so uh  well thank you so much dr roy and dr  hendricks  for your time today do you have any  final comments for our audience  and friends people yeah yes  if people have any questions related to  the uh to the top tracker system  or our experience together with uh if  you know any systems then  yeah feel free to reach out through the  website or  i guess uh through uh maybe through the  chat  well thank you again dr roy and dr  hendricks for your time today  and for your important research we would  also like to thank  lab roots and our sponsor milton yi  biotech for underwriting today's  educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration this webcast  can be viewed on demand  lab roots will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

